Third parties
-------------

ffmpeg / ffprobe
  Not shipped with this package. If you point it at a static build (the gyan.dev
  "essentials" builds used while developing are GPLv3 because of the bundled
  libx264/libx265), the binary keeps its own license and both the source-offer
  and redistribution obligations are yours to meet. The plugin only executes the
  binary; it does not link against libav*.

dsh-video-storyboard  (prior art, with thanks)
  https://github.com/SAOS-3624/dsh-video-storyboard — MIT, Copyright (c) 2026
  SAOS-3624. The idea this package starts from — one contact sheet with burnt-in
  cell indices, plus a text index binding cells to timecodes, assembled without
  an image library — comes from that project, and its documentation is the
  clearest statement of why the approach works. Thank you.
  This package is an independent implementation: no code is copied from it, and
  its detection, cell budgeting, decoding and composition are different. It is
  deliberately wire-compatible (same three tool names, same argument shapes, same
  manifest grammar) so it can stand in where the original is already installed.

Benchmark corpus (bench/corpus/)
  Synthetic, generated by bench/gen/ from numpy/Pillow shaders and rendered text.
  No third-party footage is redistributed. The audio of clip G was synthesised
  with the Windows SAPI desktop voice on the development machine and is included
  for testing only. Clip D's on-screen strings are ordinary product-description
  sentences written for this test. All CJK glyphs in the corpus were rendered
  with a Windows system font into PNG pixel data; no font file is distributed.
